.TH TIFF2ICNS 1 "September 25, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBtiff2icns\fR \- convert an image file into an icns icon family file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tiff2icns
[\fB\-noLarge\fR] \fIinfile\fR [\fIoutfile\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tiff2icns
reads an image and writes an
.B .icns
icon family built from the square representations the source already carries.
The family is written in the order an icon family is expected to hold them:
48, 32, 16, 128, 256 and 512 pixels.
.PP
A representation is used only when it is exactly
.I size
by
.I size
pixels; a representation of some other shape, including one that merely spans
.I size
in one direction, is not used at all.  When several representations of the
same size qualify, the one with the most bits per sample is used, whatever
order the source stores them in.  Sizes the source does not provide are left
out, so the result can be much smaller than a complete icon set.  Everything
that is written is stored with 8 bits per sample, so a representation read
from a deeper source is converted rather than rejected.
.PP
A 1024 by 1024 representation is never written, because an icon family does
not hold one.  It is still recognized, and it is enough to satisfy
.BR \-noLarge .
No 32 by 32 representation is synthesized either: a source that has no
square representation of a size the family uses produces no entry for it.
.PP
If
.I outfile
is omitted, the output path is the source path with its last extension
replaced by
.BR icns ,
so
.I icon.tiff
becomes
.IR icon.icns .
The file is written atomically.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-noLarge
Report a source that has no representation for any of the sizes above.  The
option changes nothing about what is written: a 128, 256 or 512 pixel
representation is written whether or not it is given.  It is recognized only
as the first argument, so any later occurrence of the word is taken as the
output file name instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B tiff2icns
exits 0 when the icon family was written, which includes the case of an empty
family, and 1 when the source does not exist, cannot be read as an image, or
the output cannot be written.
.PP
A source that loads but holds no representation of any expected size is not an
error.  Without
.B \-noLarge
nothing is said and an empty file is written; with it, a warning naming the
output file is printed first.  Either way the exit status is 0.
.PP
A file that exists but is not an image, including an empty file and a
directory, is reported as a source that cannot be loaded.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert an image, letting the output name be derived from the input:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiff2icns icon.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Convert to a chosen name, and be told when the source holds nothing that the
icon family can use:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiff2icns -noLarge icon.tiff Icon.icns
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iconutil (1),
.BR sips (1),
.BR tiffutil (1)
